🔹 thread.start()

Creates a new thread in the JVM.

The run() method executes in a separate call stack (parallel execution).

Correct way to start a thread.

Example:

class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Running in: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t1 = new MyThread();
        t1.start();  // starts a new thread
        System.out.println("Main thread: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}


Output (order may vary):

Main thread: main
Running in: Thread-0




🔹 thread.run()

Does not create a new thread.

Simply calls the run() method like a normal method.

Executes in the current thread’s call stack (usually main).

Example:

MyThread t1 = new MyThread();
t1.run();   // behaves like a normal method call, no new thread


Output:

Running in: main


✅ Summary:

start() → Spawns a new thread, runs run() in parallel.

run() → Just a normal method call, no multithreading.